---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="FAQ" description="How this site works and how to contribute.">
  <h1>FAQ</h1>

  <section>
    <h2>What is this site?</h2>
    <p>
      A collection of open problems in mathematical logic &mdash; set theory, model theory, proof
      theory, computability theory, and related areas. Each problem has its own page with a precise
      statement, its current status, definitions of the terms it uses, known partial results, and
      references.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What do the statuses mean?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Open</strong> &mdash; unresolved, as far as we know.</li>
      <li><strong>Closed</strong> &mdash; resolved, with a generally accepted proof.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claimed proof, no consensus</strong> &mdash; a proof has been claimed but is not yet
        broadly verified or accepted.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What do the exclamation marks (difficulty/importance) mean?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>!</strong> &mdash; notable, but below the bar for !! or !!!.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>!!</strong> &mdash; substantial: the kind of result that could be published in a
        strong journal (Advances-level or above).
      </li>
      <li><strong>!!!</strong> &mdash; award-level significance.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">This rubric is inherently subjective &mdash; treat it as a rough guide, not a strict rule.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What problems can be included?</h2>
    <p>
      As a rule of thumb, a problem should have appeared in a published paper or book. We make
      exceptions for problems appearing only in preprints or other unpublished sources, provided a
      brief justification is given &mdash; this is asked for directly when you
      <a href="/problems/new">submit a new problem</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do I suggest an edit to a problem?</h2>
    <p>
      Every problem page has an editor where you can revise its details and prose, add a short
      description of your change, and submit it for review. You do not need a GitHub account.
      Changes are reviewed by the maintainer before they go live.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do I propose a new problem?</h2>
    <p>
      Use <a href="/problems/new">Submit a New Problem</a>. You'll fill in the same kind of
      structured details (area, difficulty, reference, etc.) plus a note on where the problem was
      published. It goes through the same review process as edits before appearing on the site.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Can I comment on a problem?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes &mdash; each problem page has a comment section. A lightweight sign-up (no GitHub account
      needed) is required to post.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
